Suppose we have $k\geq 3$ piles of tokens.  Let $m=\lceil\frac{k}{3}\rceil$. Suppose each pile has more than $m$ tokens.  Then it is a $\mathcal{P}$-position precisely when:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $k$ odd, then the number of odd piles is even
    \item If $k=0 \bmod 4$, then the number of odd piles is $0$, $2$, $4$,  \ldots $\frac{k}{2}-2$,   $\frac{k}{2}+1$ \ldots, $k-5$, $k-3$, $k-1$.
    \item If $k=2 \bmod 4$, then the number of odd piles is $0$, $2$, $4$,  \ldots $\frac{k}{2}-1$,   $\frac{k}{2}+2$ \ldots, $k-5$, $k-3$, $k-1$.
\end{itemize}

