\label{con:refined}
Let $J_n(w)$ be a finite Jacobi pencil and let
\[
\chi_n(\lambda,w)=\det(\lambda I+J_n(w)).
\]
Every reducible factorization of $\chi_n$ is expected to be obtained by
iterating the following operations on connected blocks:
\begin{enumerate}
\item cutting the chain at a zero coupling $b_i=0$;
\item extracting a constant branch $\lambda+a_j$ from a connected block for
which $P_I(-a_j,t)\equiv0$;
\item decomposing a palindromic connected block into its symmetric and
anti-symmetric parts under reversal;
\item decomposing a scalar-diagonal connected block $a_r=\cdots=a_s$ by
factoring the characteristic polynomial of its constant off-diagonal part.
\end{enumerate}
After all these operations have been exhausted, the remaining factors are
expected to be irreducible in $\C[\lambda,w]$.

\label{conj:lk-tangles}
The statement of Theorem \ref{lc-conj}, adapted for tangle mosaics, holds.

Precisely, let $t_1$ and $t_2$ be two tame tangles, and let $T_1$ and $T_2$ be two arbitrarily chosen mosaic representatives of $t_1$ and $t_2$, respectively. Then $t_1$ and $t_2$ are of the same tangle type if and only if, after applying finitely many tangle injections to place the representatives in a common dimension if necessary, the resulting mosaics are related by a finite sequence of mosaic Reidemeister moves.

