{\label{conj-low-deg}}
    For ``natural'' high-dimensional hypothesis testing problems between $\Pb$ and $\Qb$, the following statements hold.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(1)] If $\mathsf{Adv}_{\leq D}\big( \tfrac{\mathrm{d}\Pb'}{\mathrm{d}\Qb'} \big)=O(1)$ as $n\to\infty$ for some $\Pb',\Qb'$ such that $\operatorname{TV}(\Pb,\Pb'), \operatorname{TV}(\Qb,\Qb')=o(1)$, then there exists a constant $C$ such that no algorithm with running time $n^{D/(\log n)^C}$ that achieves strong detection between $\Pb$ and $\Qb$. 
        \item[(2)] If $\mathsf{Adv}_{\leq D}\big( \tfrac{\mathrm{d}\Pb'}{\mathrm{d}\Qb'} \big)=1+o(1)$ as $n\to\infty$ for some $\Pb',\Qb'$ such that $\operatorname{TV}(\Pb,\Pb'), \operatorname{TV}(\Qb,\Qb')=o(1)$, then there exists a constant $C$ such that no algorithm with running time $n^{D/(\log n)^C}$ that achieves weak detection between $\Pb$ and $\Qb$. 
    \end{enumerate}

\label{conj-AJTmorebase}
Let $k \geq 3$ be an integer. Then there exists a threshold $C(k)$ such that if $p > C(k)$, and we have
$k$ nonsingular matrices $M_1, \dots, M_{k}$ over $\mathbb{F}_p$, then there is a vector $x$ such that each of the vectors $M_ix$ ($1\leq i\leq k$) have only nonzero entries.
